NAME
    cc - the C compiler (y1cc), on the machine

SYNOPSIS
    cc prog.c [-o prog.asm] [--org N] [--os] [--xisa] [--stack N]
       [--boot] [--vector] [--no-brur] [-l]

DESCRIPTION
    Compiles a C program (y1cc's subset: man pages cannot hold it, see
    /DOCS/Y1CC.MD) to YACC1 assembly, the same text y1cc.py writes on
    the Mac (only the date in its first line differs: Y1/OS has no
    clock, it says 0000-00-00 00:00). asm then makes the program:

        cc HELLO.C -o HELLO.ASM --org 0x5000 --os
        asm HELLO.ASM
        HELLO

    --org 0x5000 --os is what a Y1/OS program needs (the program area
    starts at $5000; putchar and getchar go through the shell, so > and
    | work). -o names the output (default: the source's name with .asm).

    The compiler is nine programs, /LIB/CC/CC1 to CC9, run one after
    the other: cc starts CC1, and each pass EXECs the next one (they
    replace each other, one command). An error stops the chain with its
    message on the screen. The passes pass their work in files
    CCW.OPT ... CCW.EM in the current directory, which stay there and
    are replaced by the next compile (pack gets the space back).
    #include "name" is looked for beside the including file, then in
    /LIB (upper case: /LIB/Y1LIB.C); the runtime is /LIB/Y1CCRT.TXT.

LIMITS
    The passes' tables (software/compiler/c/ylim): about 760 names, 170
    functions, 380 variables, 920 nodes in a function; eight nested
    #includes. The command line is 122 characters. Slow: hello.c is
    about 6M instructions, a 400-line program 100M+ (minutes to an
    hour at 1 MHz).

EXAMPLES
    cc /SRC/FIB.C -o FIB.ASM --org 0x5000 --os
    cc HELLO.C -l                      the -l summary line

NOTES
    New in Y1/OS 2026-09-25, with the syscalls EXEC and EXIT (man fs).

SEE ALSO
    asm, run, fs, abi
